Context & Problem Statement
Currently, our parallel AI development cycles suffer from deployment collisions where concurrent feature branch deployments overwrite or interfere with each other in shared staging or development environments.

However, traditional approaches to ephemeral environments (such as provisioning a unique subdomain per PR, e.g., pr-123.api.company.com) introduce massive operational overhead:
Upstream services require continuous domain whitelisting updates.
Frontend CORS configurations break or require dynamic origin echoing.
Managing dozens of distinct TLS certificates and DNS records complicates CI/CD.
2. Proposed Solution
We propose implementing a Context-Aware Routing Layer using our existing AWS Application Load Balancer (ALB). This preserves a single, static domain (api.company.com) while allowing developers to spin up completely isolated backend workloads mapped dynamically via request identifiers.

Key Mechanism
Routing Identifiers: Requests are matched using either:
HTTP Header: X-Environment-ID: pr-123
Query Parameter: ?env=pr-123
Dynamic Target Groups: When a PR opens, CI/CD provisions a dedicated target group, registers the ephemeral ECS task or EKS pod, and creates temporary ALB listener rules.
Baseline Protection: The develop branch pipeline remains strictly decoupled, updating only the stable production/staging baseline target group. Feature branches have zero access to the baseline infrastructure.
3. Workflow Architecture
A. Develop Branch Merge (Baseline Deployment)
Code is pushed/merged directly into the develop branch.
CI/CD triggers a rolling update to the stable ECS/EKS service.
The Baseline Target Group is updated with the new stable containers. Feature branch environments cannot touch this path.
B. Feature Branch Pull Request (Ephemeral Environment)
A pull request is opened against develop.
CI/CD builds the AI service and spawns an isolated ECS task or EKS pod.
A unique target group (pr-<PR_NUM>-tg) is provisioned.
The ephemeral workload IP is registered to the target group.
Two ALB listener rules are programmatically added:
Rule 1 (Header match): X-Environment-ID: pr-<PR_NUM> → Forward to ephemeral target group.
Rule 2 (Query param match): env=pr-<PR_NUM> → Forward to ephemeral target group.
Developers test locally or via browser extension headers against the standard static domain (api.company.com).
C. Teardown Lifecycle
Primary Trigger: When a PR is closed or merged, the CI/CD pipeline executes a teardown script that deletes the specific ALB listener rules, deletes the dynamic target group, and terminates the underlying container workload.
Safety Net: A scheduled daily cleanup job sweeps for orphaned target groups or rules tagged with ManagedBy: CICD exceeding a 48-hour TTL.
4. Advantages Over Alternative Approaches
Zero Upstream Friction: Upstream service providers and CORS policies remain untouched because the public-facing domain never changes.
Cost Efficiency: No heavy, fully duplicated duplicate environments are running 24/7; only active PR workloads consume compute resources.
Low Infrastructure Complexity: By leveraging native AWS ALB listener rules, we avoid the heavy operational overhead of introducing external proxies (like Traefik/Envoy) or complex service meshes (like Istio) at our current scale.
5. Risks, Mitigations & Limits
ALB Listener Rule Limit: AWS enforces a hard limit of 100 listener rules per listener.
Mitigation: At a peak of 10 concurrent PRs, we will consume roughly 20 rules (2 rules per PR), keeping us safely at 20% capacity. If our concurrency scales beyond 40+ PRs in the future, we will graduate to an Envoy proxy architecture.
Orphaned Resources: Failed pipeline deletions could leave stale rules.
Mitigation: Strict resource tagging (PRNumber, ManagedBy) combined with automated cron-based garbage collection.
